" (https://intrusion-detection.distrinet-research.be/WTMC2021/Resources/wtmc2021_Engelen_Troubleshooting.pdf)\nciteturn8view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 6; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://intrusion-detection.distrinet-research.be/WTMC2021/Resources/wtmc2021_Engelen_Troubleshooting.pdf\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 602\nL0@P0: Troubleshooting an Intrusion Detection Dataset:\nL1@P0: the CICIDS2017 Case Study\nL2@P0: Gints Engelen, Vera Rimmer, and Wouter Joosen\nL3@P0: imec-DistriNet, KU Leuven\nL4@P0: Leuven, Belgium\nL5@P0: Email:{firstname.lastname}@kuleuven.be\nL6@P0: Abstract—Numerous studies have demonstrated the effective\u0002ness of machine learning techniques in application to network\nL7@P0: intrusion detection. And yet, the adoption of machine learning\nL8@P0: for securing large-scale network environments remains chal\u0002lenging. The community acknowledges that network security\nL9@P0: presents unique challenges for machine learning, and the lack\nL10@P0: of training data representative of modern traffic remains one\nL11@P0: of the most intractable issues. New attempts are continuously\nL12@P0: made to develop high quality benchmark datasets and proper\nL13@P0: data collection methodologies. The CICIDS2017 dataset is one\nL14@P0: of the recent results, created to meet the demanding criterion of\nL15@P0: representativeness for network intrusion detection.\nL16@P0: In this paper we revisit CICIDS2017 and its data collection\nL17@P0: pipeline and analyze correctness, validity and overall utility of\nL18@P0: the dataset for the learning task. During this in-depth analysis,\nL19@P0: we uncover a series of problems with traffic generation, flow\nL20@P0: construction, feature extraction and labelling that severely affect\nL21@P0: the aforementioned properties. We investigate the causes of these\nL22@P0: shortcomings and address most of them by applying an improved\nL23@P0: data processing methodology. As a result, more than 20 percent\nL24@P0: of original traffic traces are reconstructed or relabelled. Machine\nL25@P0: learning benchmarks on the final dataset demonstrate significant\nL26@P0: improvements. Our study exemplifies how data collection issues\nL27@P0: may have enormous impact on model evaluation and provides\nL28@P0: recommendations for their anticipation and prevention.\nL29@P0: Index Terms—network intrusion detection, machine learning,\nL30@P0: benchmark dataset, data collection.\nL31@P0: I. INTRODUCTION\nL32@P0: Network intrusion detection systems (NIDS) are security\nL33@P0: tools strategically deployed in a network environment that\nL34@P0: monitor and interpret internal and external traffic in search\nL35@P0: for malicious activities. Advances in Machine Learning (ML)\nL36@P0: over the last decades have enabled NIDS to evolve from simple\nL37@P0: rule-based systems to intelligent automated decision-making\nL38@P0: engines powered by modern learning algorithms [1]. Despite\nL39@P0: significant progress in ML-based defensive research, a lot of\nL40@P0: challenges are still open when it comes to NIDS solutions. For\nL41@P0: any data-driven technique, the role of high quality, representa\u0002tive datasets cannot be underestimated; this is especially true\nL42@P0: for NIDS research that has to account for high non-stationarity\nL43@P0: and strong heterogeneity of network traffic, together with an\nL44@P0: uncontrollable production environment.\nL45@P0: In NIDS research, evaluation on benchmark datasets pri\u0002marily serves a two-fold purpose: (i) real-world performance\nL46@P0: estimation of a particular algorithm, and (ii) consistent com\u0002parison between different approaches. In this respect, quality\nL47@P0: of data has a decisive influence on valid outcomes of both\nL48@P0: objectives. Research has shown that many benchmark datasets\nL49@P0: do not adequately represent the real problem of network in\u0002trusion detection, discrediting performance numbers achieved\nL50@P0: in laboratory conditions [1]. As a response, over the last 10\nL51@P0: years the community has collectively devised the criteria that\nL52@P0: reliable research traffic data should meet [2]–[4]. These criteria\nL53@P0: can be seen as objectives of proper dataset generation, be that\nL54@P0: for capturing real traffic or simulating attack scenarios.\nL55@P0: Recently, the Canadian Institute of Cybersecurity released\nL56@P0: a new dataset – CICIDS2017 [5] – with the aim to address\nL57@P0: some of the issues that plagued older datasets. CICIDS2017 is\nL58@P0: comprised of real traffic generated through simulated interac\u0002tions between hosts in a controlled infrastructure, which means\nL59@P0: that the resulting network traffic retains basic limitations of a\nL60@P0: simulated environment. Nevertheless, the authors do a great\nL61@P0: effort to achieve heterogeneity, diversity and completeness\nL62@P0: of the dataset in order to maximize its utility for research\nL63@P0: purposes. Owing to that promise, CICIDS2017 is growing\nL64@P0: in usage in NIDS research [6]–[9]. Crucially, however, the\nL65@P0: dataset is largely taken at its face value, assuming that a\nL66@P0: modern dataset collected with the quality requirements in\nL67@P0: mind indeed satisfies these criteria. As a result, the studies\nL68@P0: use successful ML evaluations as a sole confirmation of\nL69@P0: correctness of CICIDS2017, but do not explicitly validate these\nL70@P0: results by examining the data. The lack of dedicated analysis\nL71@P0: sheds uncertainty on the empirical and theoretical research\nL72@P0: results obtained when leveraging the dataset. We have revisited\nL73@P0: the properties of CICIDS2017 as a NIDS dataset and analyse\nL74@P0: to what extent it satisfies the declared requirements.\nL75@P0: Our contributions can be summarized as follows:\nL76@P0: • We perform a thorough methodological manual analysis\nL77@P0: of the raw CICIDS2017 dataset and its respective feature\nL78@P0: extraction tool in order to review the dataset creation\nL79@P0: pipeline, consisting of attack simulation, flow construc\u0002tion, labelling, feature extraction and ML benchmarking.\nL80@P0: Our investigation reveals errors in attack simulation,\nL81@P0: feature extraction, labelling and benchmarking of the\nL82@P0: dataset. Moreover, we find out that more than 25% of all\nL83@P0: flows in the dataset turn out to be meaningless artefacts\nL84@P0: carrying no real identifying characteristics. For several\nL85@P0: attack categories, their fraction reaches 50%, which is\nL86@P0: significantly beyond an acceptable noise level. Further\nL87@P0: investigation confirms that the issue is still present in the\nL88@P0-1: newest version of the dataset - CIC-CSE-DS2018.• We analyse the negative impact of the dataset errors,\nL89@P1: artefacts and mislabelled traces on ML algorithms, and\nL90@P1: provide insights into why these issues can easily pass\nL91@P1: undetected when using the dataset for NIDS research.\nL92@P1: • Building on these findings, we modify the feature extrac\u0002tor and regenerate and relabel the CICIDS2017 dataset\nL93@P1: based on the original PCAPs1. Improved correctness and\nL94@P1: reliability of the regenerated dataset are further supported\nL95@P1: by ML benchmarks that reach the highest performance\nL96@P1: ever reported on these data.\nL97@P1: II. DATASET & RELATED WORK\nL98@P1: A NIDS dataset is considered reliable when it covers the\nL99@P1: following properties. It should contain (a) real and (b) valid\nL100@P1: network traffic, such that it constitutes complete scenarios that\nL101@P1: can be observed in actual production environments. The benign\nL102@P1: and malicious traces should be (c) correctly labelled, (d)\nL103@P1: highly variant/diverse in terms of represented services, client\nL104@P1: behaviours and attacks, (e) correctly implemented, according\nL105@P1: to commonly accepted standards, and (f) easily updatable,\nL106@P1: when new services and attacks are introduced. The dataset\nL107@P1: should also be (g) reproducible, enabling comparison across\nL108@P1: different datasets, and (h) shareable, containing no private\nL109@P1: information. Finally, a good dataset should be supplied with\nL110@P1: (i) documentation and metadata of data collection, including\nL111@P1: details about the network infrastructure and simulated attack\nL112@P1: scenarios. CICIDS2017 was created as a response to these\nL113@P1: dataset quality evaluation criteria, and thus is asserted to\nL114@P1: satisfy all of them by design.\nL115@P1: The dataset is generated in a complete network topology\nL116@P1: with diverse devices and operating systems as a testbed, where\nL117@P1: separate victim and attacker networks communicate over the\nL118@P1: Internet. To collect benign traffic that models realistic user\nL119@P1: behaviour, the authors use profiling agents [11] that are trained\nL120@P1: beforehand on network events generated through genuine\nL121@P1: human interactions in the network. Communication covers all\nL122@P1: common available protocols, such as HTTP, HTTPS, FTP,\nL123@P1: SSH and email protocols. In order to fulfil the requirement\nL124@P1: for attack diversity, the authors use the 2016 McAfee report to\nL125@P1: compose the list of most common attacks: Brute Force attacks,\nL126@P1: Heartbleed attack, Botnet communication, several variants of\nL127@P1: DoS attacks, DDoS, Infiltration attack and Web attacks. In\nL128@P1: total, the dataset comprises over 2.8 million traffic traces\nL129@P1: categorized into 15 classes. Individual traffic traces are repre\u0002sented by a feature vector of 80 extracted high-level statistical\nL130@P1: features, manually engineered based on expert knowledge of\nL131@P1: traffic characteristics relevant to intrusion detection.\nL132@P1: Since 2017, plenty of studies have deployed CICIDS2017\nL133@P1: (and its latest version CSE-CIC-IDS2018) for NIDS re\u0002search [6]–[9]. The studied research problems span ML\u0002assisted network intrusion detection, novelty detection,\nL134@P1: anomaly detection, online detection, with a large variety of\nL135@P1: learning algorithms and traffic representations – raw PCAPs,\nL136@P1: 1The regenerated dataset and modified feature extractor can be found at\nL137@P1: downloads.distrinet-research.be/WTMC2021 [10], where we also provide a\nL138@P1: more low-level documentation of all our changes.\nL139@P1: statistical features, graphs, etc. Recently, both datasets started\nL140@P1: being considered for adversarial ML research, such as evasion\nL141@P1: of botnet detection [12]. The conclusions reached by these\nL142@P1: studies largely rely on correctness and validity of the dataset\nL143@P1: in all its forms. There are studies that have analyzed features\nL144@P1: used in the dataset [13] and raised the issue of class imbal\u0002ance [14]. However, these analyses do not address correctness\nL145@P1: and validity of the dataset – the properties that are strongly\nL146@P1: required to support any research results obtained from the data.\nL147@P1: III. MANUAL ANALYSIS AND CORRECTIONS\nL148@P1: We select CICIDS2017 for in-depth analysis based not\nL149@P1: only on its growing adoption in the literature, but also on\nL150@P1: sufficient transparency and reproducibility of its generation\nL151@P1: process. This section describes the main analysis of the dataset\nL152@P1: and its feature extraction tool. Our goal here is to revise the\nL153@P1: aforementioned properties of a good NIDS dataset, which we\nL154@P1: do through examining the following dataset creation stages:\nL155@P1: • Attack simulation. Most research datasets for NIDS con\u0002tain malicious traces representative of target malicious\nL156@P1: activities. To include a wide variety of attacks, and save\nL157@P1: the effort of annotating real traffic, the attacks are often\nL158@P1: simulated with open source tools or custom scripts.\nL159@P1: • Flow construction. Raw network traffic is a continuous\nL160@P1: data stream that needs to be monitored and analyzed in\nL161@P1: real time. It is therefore necessary to decide what the\nL162@P1: level of granularity will be, i.e. what constitutes a single\nL163@P1: input unit for the detection system. Traffic is commonly\nL164@P1: processed at granularity of a flow associated with one\nL165@P1: complete network connection.\nL166@P1: • Labelling. When working with a supervised detection\nL167@P1: system, the aim is not only to distinguish between be\u0002nign and malicious traffic, but to label malicious traffic\nL168@P1: according to the attack class that it belongs to. When\nL169@P1: training an ML-based classifier for this purpose, it needs\nL170@P1: to be supplied with example network data from all classes\nL171@P1: that it needs to be able to detect, along with the correct\nL172@P1: label for each input. When testing the model, comparing\nL173@P1: correct labels with predicted classes allows to assess the\nL174@P1: model’s performance.\nL175@P1: • Feature extraction. Traditional ML systems cannot op\u0002erate on multidimensional raw traffic data, and instead\nL176@P1: require some form of structured input. Therefore, a flow\u0002based intrusion detection system would convert each\nL177@P1: raw traffic flow to a fixed-length structured vector that\nL178@P1: encapsulates all the important information about the flow\nL179@P1: in a form of numerical and/or categorical features. For\nL180@P1: instance, a NIDS could extract statistical features of the\nL181@P1: flow, such as total flow duration, total forward pack\u0002ets, average inter-packet arrival time, maximum packet\nL182@P1: length, and others.\nL183@P1: • ML benchmarks. Since the dataset is meant for design\nL184@P1: and optimization of data-driven network security solu\u0002tions, the final stage of its creation is performing ML\nL185@P1: benchmarks. Benchmarking includes designing, training\nL186@P1-2: and testing one or more ML models on the constructeddataset for a lab evaluation of network intrusion detection.\nL187@P2: This stage is meant to confirm that the dataset and its\nL188@P2: features are meaningful and appropriate for ML-based\nL189@P2: NIDS, and optionally to compare performance of various\nL190@P2: algorithms.\nL191@P2: Understanding the underlying mechanisms of dataset gen\u0002eration enables researchers and practitioners to properly as\u0002sess relevance of the data to the problem at hand, foresee\nL192@P2: potential pitfalls of applying ML to these data, and essentially\nL193@P2: set correct expectations. Dataset documentation is crucial in\nL194@P2: facilitating this analysis, and in case of CICIDS2017 we use\nL195@P2: several sources of documentation: (i) the original paper [5], (ii)\nL196@P2: the website page describing the dataset and its features [15],\nL197@P2: and (iii) the github page of the feature extraction tool [16].\nL198@P2: In the following, we describe our analysis process for each\nL199@P2: dataset generation stage of interest, in the order applied during\nL200@P2: our study, and report our findings.\nL201@P2: A. Flow construction\nL202@P2: CICIDS2017 flows are constructed from raw PCAP files\nL203@P2: using the CICFlowMeter tool [16] by the same authors, which\nL204@P2: outputs CSV files where each row corresponds to a flow,\nL205@P2: and each flow has 83 (excluding the label) features. They\nL206@P2: define a flow as a bidirectional exchange of network packets\nL207@P2: belonging to the same 5-tuple – a unique set {source IP ad\u0002dress, destination IP address, source port, destination port,\nL208@P2: transport layer protocol} – within a certain time period.\nL209@P2: 1) TCP “appendices”. We analyze the source code of\nL210@P2: CICFlowMeter to understand how each flow is recorded. A\n"
